Both items are closed and verified. One of them was bigger than its record said, and the pass surfaced something bigger than either.

## Item 1 — annex change-log fragment, column T

The brief named one cell. A sweep of all nine specs, every sheet, every cell, found **three**:

| Cell | Zone | Before → After |
|---|---|---|
| `Dimona Spec!T55` | 01231 | 348 → 92 chars |
| `DmfA Spec!T221` | 01252 | 356 → 238 chars |
| `DmfA Modification Spec!T221` | 01252 | 356 → 238 chars |

The two extras are the identical parser bug in the identical column (`DMFA-Annexe numéro1` + `AANN22002244--11--FFRR11..ppddff`). I fixed them rather than leave a known twin behind a "final" pass, and root-caused all three in `dimona_glossary.json` / `dmfa_glossary.json`.

**Content-loss proof, per cell:** the after-text is a character-for-character *prefix* of the before-text; the only dropped substring is the annex block; every published anomaly code survives.

**Relocation decided on the merits, not by reflex.** The Dimona fragment carried a real fact — Annexe 5 *Liste des codes pays* was amended at edition 2026/2, published 01/06/2026 — and that annex is genuinely consumed here (cited by Option Values on zones 00035, 00119, 00169, 00523), so it moved to a Version Control REMARKS row. The DmfA fragment announced Annexe 1 *Codes communes – Code INS*, which **no** DmfA cell cites (they cite annexe 23), and carried no amendment claim at all. There was nothing worth relocating, so I invented nothing and recorded the removal instead.

## Item 2 — BE-X10-015 root cause, zero drift

29 source fields corrected, **not one delivered cell written by this part**. Your counts were exact; the sweep also found 3 polluted `block_xml` fields no record had named — dead data (only `build_06`/`build_07` read `zone['block_xml']`; DmfA uses its `blocks` table), cleaned as hygiene. `name_fr` was re-derived from the held PDFs by the same extraction the original patch used — 10/10 reproduced the delivered cells before anything was written.

**The zero-drift proof.** Builders write in-place, so I ran all nine in an isolated sandbox before *and* after the fix. BE-X10-015's cell set was defined mechanically — every cell whose *rebuild* value carried a marker — giving **37 cells** (DmfA 12, DmfA Mod 12, DRS 13).

- **37/37 delivered values UNCHANGED** vs pre-pass backups
- **37/37 now reproduced exactly by a rebuild**
- Total drift **3203 → 3172**, reconciling exactly as −37 markers +6 new VC cells
- **6 of 9 workbooks byte-identical**, including `BE-NSSO-004-DRS-Spec.xlsx`, the one most implicated
- The 3 that moved moved by **9 cells total**; style/fill/font/width/freeze drift outside the new rows: **0**

